{
  "title": "OS-HARM: A Benchmark for Measuring Safety of Computer Use Agents",
  "authors": [
    "Thomas Kuntz",
    "Agatha Duzan",
    "Hao Zhao",
    "Francesco Croce",
    "Zico Kolter",
    "Nicolas Flammarion",
    "Maksym Andriushchenko"
  ],
  "year": 2025,
  "arxivId": "2506.14866",
  "abstract": "The safety of computer use agents, which can interact with a graphical user interface, has been largely overlooked. To address this gap, we introduce OS-HARM, a benchmark for measuring the safety of these agents across three categories of harm: deliberate user misuse, prompt injection attacks, and model misbehavior. Our benchmark provides a valuable testbed for evaluating agent safety and alignment across realistic computer use tasks.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "Computer use agents can pose significant safety risks if they are not designed with safety in mind. <strong>Deliberate user misuse</strong>, <strong>prompt injection attacks</strong>, and <strong>model misbehavior</strong> are three categories of harm that these agents can cause. OS-HARM is a benchmark that tests agents across these categories to evaluate their safety.",
      "visualization": {
